# Coil — Screens, the HUD, and audio

This file fixes the screens the game moves between, what each one shows, the HUD
that sits above the board, and the audio cues. The actions the screens are driven
by are in `specs/controls.md`, the board they frame is in `specs/board.md`, and
the figures they report are in `specs/scoring.md`. Every piece of screen copy
below carries the name this specification gives it.

## Presentation

Coil fixes no palette, no font, and no artwork. `specs/overview.md` states what a
player has to read at a glance, and how the game looks is yours. One requirement
is this file's own: every piece of text a screen shows is legible against
whatever sits behind it at the logical stage size `STAGE_W x STAGE_H`
(`1280 x 720`).

## The screens

The snapshot's `screen` field, fixed in `specs/instrumentation.md`, names the
screen the game is in, and the game opens on `title`.

| `screen` | The screen |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | The title and main menu. |
| `howto` | How to play. |
| `playing` | The live round. |
| `paused` | The pause menu over a frozen round. |
| `gameover` | The round ended by a fatal collision. |
| `cleared` | The round ended by clearing the board. |

`title`, `howto`, `paused`, `gameover`, and `cleared` carry a menu, and
`menuIndex` is the highlighted item on each of them. The highlighted item
is drawn distinctly from the others, so a player always sees which item `confirm`
would accept. Arriving at `howto`, `paused`, `gameover`, or `cleared` sets
`menuIndex` to `0`, and arriving at `title` sets it to `titleIndex`, as Menu
input below states. On `playing` the highlight is not shown and `menuIndex` rests
at `0`.

### `title`

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `COIL` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `GRID SERPENT` |
| Best score | `BEST_LABEL` | `BEST`, above the session's best score |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | The mode's entry, then `HOW TO PLAY` |

`specs/mode.md` fixes the mode's entry, which is the first item. `confirm` on it
starts a round, which sets `screen` to `playing` with the board laid out as
`specs/board.md` states. `confirm` on `HOW TO PLAY` sets `screen` to `howto`.
`back` does nothing here.

The board furniture may show behind the menu, dimmed or otherwise quieted, if
that suits the look you design.

### `howto`

How to play, written in a player's words rather than as rules of a system. It
covers:

- that the snake never stops, and that steering turns it on the next tick;
- that the snake may only turn perpendicular to the way it is travelling, so it
  cannot double back on itself;
- that eating a pellet makes the snake one cell longer;
- that the wall border and the snake's own body are fatal to the head;
- that eating pellets in quick succession builds the combo multiplier, and that
  the multiplier falls back to one when the window runs out;
- the controls, naming the keys bound to each action in `specs/controls.md`.

`back` returns to `title`.

### `playing`

The live round, and the only screen the simulation advances on. It shows the
board, its wall border, whatever obstacle cells the mode places, the snake, the
live pellet, and the HUD.

### `paused`

Reached from `playing`, and the round is frozen behind it: no tick runs while the
game is paused, so the snake is exactly where it stood and the combo window is
exactly as full when the game resumes. The board is visible behind the pause
menu.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Menu | `PAUSE_ITEMS` | `RESUME`, `RESTART`, `MENU` |

`confirm` on `RESUME` returns to `playing` with the round untouched. `RESTART`
starts a fresh round in the same mode. `MENU` returns to `title`. `back` and
`pause` both return to `playing`, exactly as `RESUME` does.

### `gameover` and `cleared`

Both are reached when a round ends, as `specs/movement.md` states, and they are
the same screen under two headings.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Heading, on `gameover` | `GAMEOVER_TEXT` | `GAME OVER` |
| Heading, on `cleared` | `CLEARED_TEXT` | `BOARD CLEARED` |
| Final score | `SCORE_LABEL` | `SCORE`, above the score the round ended on |
| Best score | `BEST_LABEL` | `BEST`, above the session's best score |
| Menu | `OVER_ITEMS` | `PLAY AGAIN`, `MENU` |

`confirm` on `PLAY AGAIN` starts a fresh round in the same mode. `MENU` returns
to `title`. `back` returns to `title`. The final frame of the round may show
behind the panel.

## Menu input

Every menu-bearing screen is driven by the keyboard, by a pointer, and by touch,
over the items that screen shows. `specs/controls.md` fixes the keyboard actions
and what each does on a menu, and each screen's section above fixes what
`confirm` accepts there.

### Pointer and touch

Each menu item occupies a rectangular hit region the build lays out.
`specs/instrumentation.md` states the reading the build reports an item's region
through, in the logical units of `specs/overview.md`, so the menus keep the
layout the build chose.

| Input | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| A pointer moves onto an item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index. |
| A touch contact lands inside an item's region, or travels onto one | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index. |
| A pointer is pressed and released inside one item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index, and that item is confirmed. |
| A touch contact lands and lifts inside one item's region | `menuIndex` becomes that item's index, and that item is confirmed. |

A confirm takes both of its edges inside one item's region: the press and its
release for a pointer, the landing and the lift for a touch contact. Two edges
that fall in different regions, and an edge that falls outside every region,
confirm no item. The item every confirm acts on is the item at `menuIndex`,
whichever input raised it, and the effect is the one that screen carries for
`confirm`.

The pointer and the touch contacts are read once per frame, in the same input
read as the keyboard actions, and are applied after that frame's keyboard edges.
A press and the release that follows it may arrive on one frame, and that frame
confirms. A pointer and a touch contact drive the menus directly rather than
through an action, so neither steers the snake and neither is read on `playing`.

### The remembered title selection

`titleIndex` is the title menu's remembered selection, reported by the snapshot
`specs/instrumentation.md` fixes and opening at `0`. Confirming an item on the
title menu sets `titleIndex` to that item's index, from the keyboard, from a
pointer, and from a touch contact alike. Returning to `title` from any screen
sets `menuIndex` to `titleIndex`, so leaving `howto` lands back on `HOW TO PLAY`
and leaving a round for the title lands back on the mode entry that started it.

## What advances on each screen

`simTime` accumulates the delta time of every update on the `playing`
screen alone, and input is read on every screen.

| Screen | What advances |
| --- | --- |
| `playing` | The tick, and everything a tick resolves. |
| Every other screen | Nothing. |

## The HUD

The HUD occupies the band above the board, `y` in `[0, BOARD_Y)`. It shows four
things, spread across the band so none crowds another, and it stays clear of the
play area.

| Readout | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Score | `SCORE_LABEL` | `SCORE`, above the current score |
| Best | `BEST_LABEL` | `BEST`, above the session's best score |
| Combo | — | The multiplier as `x2` through `x5`, with a bar beneath it |
| Mode | — | The mode's short label, as `specs/mode.md` fixes it |

The combo readout and its bar are shown only while `M` is at least `2`, and the
combo area is empty at `M` of `1`. The bar drains from full to empty over the
combo window as `specs/scoring.md` states, so a player reads at a glance how much
of the window is left.

A mute indicator sits in the band as well. It reads the current sound state
unambiguously, either as a marker present only while muted or as a readout naming
both states, and the muted and unmuted HUDs are told apart from the board alone.
It changes on the frame `mute` is read, and muting changes nothing else on
screen.

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write. Play exactly the four cues in
`CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus. Define and play exactly the four cues
in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus: define exactly the four cues in `CUES`
from the game instance's `initialize` and play them from the world's audio inside
a tick, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{/if}}

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `eat` | `CUES.eat` | The snake eats a pellet. |
| `combo-up` | `CUES.comboUp` | The combo multiplier rises. |
| `death` | `CUES.death` | The head enters a fatal cell. |
| `music` | `CUES.music` | A round begins. |

The `music` bed loops under the round it began with. It keeps playing while that
round is paused, because pausing has not ended the round. Once the round has
ended the bed is stopped rather than left running quietly, so a player who dies,
clears the board, or leaves the round for the title hears no bed on the screen
they land on, and the bed sounds again only when a round next begins.

Each of the three event cues is a distinct short sound, so the three events are
told apart by ear, and each plays on the tick its event resolves, at most once on
that tick. A tick that both eats a pellet and raises the multiplier plays `eat`
and `combo-up` once each. Every cue is a file this build produces, and
`specs/assets.md` fixes how each is made.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the runtime you write. The
game binds the `mute` action to the runtime's mute bit and toggles it from any
screen, then mirrors that bit into `muted` every frame. Browsers refuse to start
audio before the player interacts with the page, so the game stays fully
playable and silent until that first interaction.
{{else}}
Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the engine. The game binds the
`mute` action to the engine's mute bit and toggles it from any screen, then
mirrors that bit into `muted` every frame. The game stays fully playable with
sound muted.
{{/if}}

## Out of scope

- Network or online play, leaderboards, and any server interaction.
- Persistence of anything between sessions.
